## Components in React: functions that return JSX
A React **component** is a JavaScript function that takes **props** (its inputs) and returns **JSX** (a description of UI that looks like HTML):
```jsx
function MenuItem({ name, price, description }) {   // props come in
  return (                                          // JSX comes out
    <div className="menu-item">
      <h3>{name}</h3>
      <p>{description}</p>
      <span>${price}</span>
    </div>
  );
}
// use it:
<MenuItem name="Latte" price={4.5} description="Espresso and steamed milk" />
```
This is *exactly* the component model from Astro, a reusable, prop-driven piece of UI, now in React. JSX is HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript: it lets you write the UI structure naturally while embedding dynamic values and expressions with `{ }` (`{name}`, `{price * 1.1}`, `{items.map(...)}`), the same `{ }` you used in Astro. React calls your function, takes the returned JSX, and renders it to the DOM.

## Composition: building UIs from component trees
The power of components is **composition**, building complex UIs by nesting components into a *tree*, which is the mental model React is named for:
```jsx
function Menu({ items }) {
  return (
    <section>
      <h2>Our Menu</h2>
      {items.map(item => <MenuItem key={item.id} {...item} />)}   // a list of components
    </section>
  );
}
function App() {
  return (<><Header /><Menu items={menu} /><Footer /></>);        // composed from pieces
}
```
Your whole UI becomes a tree: `App` contains `Header`, `Menu`, `Footer`; `Menu` contains many `MenuItem`s; and so on. This composition is the heart of the component mental model, you *decompose* a complex interface into small, focused, reusable components, each with one clear job, then *compose* them into the full UI, building complexity from simple, understandable pieces. Rendering a list (a common need) is just mapping data to components (`items.map(item => <MenuItem ... />)`), with a `key` prop on each (a unique id React uses to track list items efficiently, forgetting it is a classic beginner warning). Thinking in component trees, "this UI is a `Page` made of a `Header`, a `Sidebar`, and a `Content` made of `Card`s", is the skill that makes building any interface manageable.

## Props flow down: one-way data flow
A crucial principle of how React components relate: **data flows one way, down the tree, via props.** A parent passes data to its children through props, children receive and use that data, but a child cannot reach up and change its parent's data directly, data goes *down*, not up. This **one-way data flow** is deliberate and is what makes React apps predictable: you can always trace where a piece of data comes from (it was passed as a prop from above), so understanding why a component shows what it shows is a matter of following the props down from their source, rather than hunting through scattered code that might have changed it. It contrasts with the tangled two-way bindings of older approaches, where data could change from anywhere and tracking it down was a nightmare. (When a child *does* need to affect a parent, the parent passes down a *function* prop that the child calls, so even "upward" communication is just a prop flowing down, keeping the one-way model intact, you will use this constantly.) Understanding that props flow down and form a one-way data flow is key to reasoning about React: the UI is a tree, data descends through it as props, and that predictable flow is what keeps even large component trees comprehensible.

## Where this fits
You can build React components, functions taking props and returning JSX, and compose them into trees (decompose the UI into focused pieces, compose them into the whole), with data flowing one way down via props for predictability. This is the component mental model, now interactive. Tomorrow you add the thing that makes components come alive: state.

## Forms in React: controlled inputs
Forms are everywhere, and React handles them with **controlled components**, where the input's value is driven by state. Instead of the DOM holding the input's value (as in weeks 1-3), React state is the *single source of truth*: the input shows the state, and typing updates the state:
```jsx
function ContactForm() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [message, setMessage] = useState('');
  function handleSubmit(e) {
    e.preventDefault();                       // stop the native page reload
    // ...do something with email and message
  }
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />
      <textarea value={message} onChange={e => setMessage(e.target.value)} />
      <button>Send</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```
Each input's `value` comes from state, and its `onChange` updates that state on every keystroke, so the state *always* mirrors what is in the input. This is the declarative model applied to forms: the form's data lives in state, and the inputs are just a reflection of it.

## Why controlled inputs, and the trade-off
Controlled inputs follow directly from React's "UI as a function of state" philosophy, and the payoff is that **your component always knows the form's current values** (they are in state), which makes everything else easy: live validation (check the email as they type), enabling/disabling the submit button based on the values, transforming input (uppercase, formatting), and using the values on submit, all just read from state. The form's data and the form's display are guaranteed to be in sync because the state *is* the data and the inputs *render* it. The trade-off is some verbosity (a piece of state and an onChange per field), which for large forms is why form libraries (React Hook Form, W8) exist to reduce the boilerplate, but understanding the controlled-component model first is essential, because the libraries are built on it. There is also an *uncontrolled* approach (let the DOM hold the value, read it via a ref on submit, the W4-D4 ref idea), which is simpler for trivial forms and sometimes used, but controlled inputs are the React default because they give you the live, always-in-sync access to form values that interactive forms need. Knowing both, and that controlled is the standard, lets you choose appropriately.

## The submit handler and preventing default
A detail that trips up everyone and teaches a real lesson: the `e.preventDefault()` in the submit handler. By default, submitting a form triggers the *native* browser submission, the full-page reload to the form's `action` URL from week 3, and in a React single-page app you almost never want that (it would reload the whole app and discard your state). So you call `e.preventDefault()` to *stop* the native submission and handle the data yourself in JavaScript (validate it, send it via an API call, update state). This is a perfect callback to the native form model you learned in week 3: React forms are *built on top of* native forms, and `preventDefault` is you explicitly opting *out* of the native submit-and-reload behaviour to take control. Understanding this connects your React knowledge to the fundamentals, the native HTTP form submission is still underneath, React just intercepts it, which is exactly why learning the native model first (week 3) pays off now. Forgetting `preventDefault` causes the baffling "my form reloads the page and loses everything" bug, and knowing *why* (the native submission is firing) lets you fix it instantly. Controlled inputs plus a `preventDefault`ing submit handler is the standard React form pattern, and it rests on the form fundamentals you already have.

## Where this fits
You can build React forms with controlled components (state as the single source of truth, inputs reflect and update it) and a submit handler that `preventDefault`s the native submission to take control, the declarative model applied to forms, built on the native form fundamentals from week 3. Tomorrow you learn to keep React fast as components grow: rendering performance.

## The core distinction: server vs client components
The single most important thing to understand in the App Router is **when a component runs on the server versus the client**, because it determines what the component can do. By default, components are **server components**, but you opt into **client components** with the `"use client"` directive when you need browser-only features:
```jsx
// server component (default): runs on the server
async function MenuPage() {
  const items = await db.menu.findMany();    // direct database access - server only!
  return <Menu items={items} />;             // sends rendered HTML
}

// client component: runs in the browser
"use client";
function FilterButton() {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(false);  // useState, onClick - need the browser
  return <button onClick={() => setActive(!active)}>...</button>;
}
```
The rule: **server components for fetching data and rendering; client components for interactivity.**

## What each can and cannot do
Knowing the capabilities and limits of each is what lets you place the boundary correctly:
- **Server components** (the default): can directly access databases, the filesystem, and secrets (env vars), can be `async` to fetch data, and send HTML to the browser, but *cannot* use state (`useState`), effects, event handlers (`onClick`), or any browser API, because they do not run in the browser. They are for the *static, data-driven* parts of your UI.
- **Client components** (`"use client"`): can use state, effects, event handlers, and browser APIs (everything from last week's React), but run in the browser, so they *cannot* directly access the database or secrets (that code would be exposed). They are for the *interactive* parts.
So you cannot use `onClick` or `useState` in a server component (you get an error reminding you to add `"use client"`), and you should not try to query the database in a client component (the connection would be exposed). Each kind is suited to a different job, and the error messages guide you, "you're using state, this needs to be a client component."

## The mental model: a tree of mostly-server, islands-of-client
The architecture that makes this powerful, and the mental model to internalise, is that your app is a **tree of mostly server components with islands of client components** where interactivity is needed. You make components server by default (so most of your app benefits from server rendering, fast, SEO-friendly, secure data access) and mark *only* the genuinely interactive pieces as client components, keeping those islands as small as possible. For example, a product page is a server component (it fetches and renders the product data on the server), but the "add to cart" button inside it is a small client component (it needs `onClick`). This minimises the JavaScript sent to the browser (only the client islands ship their code) while keeping the data-fetching and rendering on the server. The discipline is to *default to server* and *reach for client only when you need interactivity* (state, events, browser APIs), pushing the `"use client"` boundary as far down the tree as possible so the interactive part is a small leaf, not a whole branch. Getting this boundary right, server for data and structure, small client islands for interactivity, is the central skill of the App Router, and it is what delivers the performance and SEO benefits while still allowing rich interactivity. Mis-placing it (making everything a client component, or trying to fetch data in a client component) loses the benefits, so understanding *which runs where and why* is the foundational App Router skill.

## Where this fits
You understand server vs client components: server by default (data access, secrets, HTML output, no interactivity) and client via `"use client"` (interactivity, browser APIs, no direct DB), with the model of a mostly-server tree with small client islands placed only where interactivity is needed. This is the central App Router distinction. Tomorrow you handle dynamic routes, pages whose content depends on the URL.

## Server actions: mutations without building an API
So far you have *fetched* and *displayed* data; **server actions** let you *change* it, submit a form, create a record, update something, by calling a server-side function *directly* from your component, with no manual API endpoint to build. You mark a function with `"use server"`, and it runs on the server when called (e.g. on form submit):
```jsx
async function createMessage(formData) {
  "use server";                              // this runs on the SERVER
  const message = formData.get('message');
  await db.messages.create({ data: { message } });   // direct database write
}
function ContactForm() {
  return (
    <form action={createMessage}>            // the form calls the server action
      <textarea name="message" />
      <button>Send</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```
When the form submits, `createMessage` runs *on the server* (with database access, secrets, all server capabilities), no fetch call, no API route, you just wrote a server function and wired the form to it.

## Why server actions are a big simplification
Server actions collapse a traditionally multi-step process into one, and understanding what they replace shows their value. The classic way to handle a form submission in a React app: write a client-side handler that `fetch`es a backend API endpoint, write that API endpoint (a separate server route) to receive the request, validate it, and write to the database, and wire the two together with the right URL, method, and JSON. Server actions eliminate most of that boilerplate: you write *one* server function and attach it to the form, and Next.js handles the wiring (the form posts to the action, the function runs on the server). This is a genuine simplification of the most common app operation, *mutating data in response to a user action*, and it keeps the logic where it belongs (the database write is in a server function with server access) without forcing you to build and maintain a separate API layer for every mutation. It also integrates with the React model: a server action can trigger a re-fetch/re-render so the UI updates after the mutation (the new message appears), giving you the full "user does something -> data changes -> UI reflects it" loop with minimal code. For the common case, a form that creates or updates data, server actions are the cleanest path.

## When server actions, and when a real API
The judgement to develop is *when* server actions fit and when you still want a traditional API endpoint (tomorrow's route handlers), because both have their place. **Server actions** are ideal for *your own app's* mutations triggered from *your own* UI, form submissions, button-triggered changes, the internal data operations of your application, where the tight integration and reduced boilerplate are exactly what you want. **Route handlers / a real API** (tomorrow) are what you need when something *other than your own React UI* must call your server: a mobile app, a third-party integration, a webhook, a public API for other developers, anything that needs a standard HTTP endpoint with a stable URL and contract. So the rule of thumb: server actions for your app's internal mutations (the common case, less boilerplate), a route handler/API for endpoints that external clients consume. This connects forward to the Backend phase (W11, REST API design): server actions are a Next.js convenience for internal mutations, while real API design (proper endpoints, methods, status codes) is for when you are exposing functionality beyond your own UI. Knowing which to reach for, the integrated server action for internal app mutations, the standalone API for external consumers, is the architectural call, and it keeps you from either over-building APIs for simple internal forms or trying to force server actions to serve external clients. For Bean Forge's own forms and data changes, server actions are the clean, modern choice.

## Where this fits
You understand server actions: server-side functions (`"use server"`) called directly from your components to mutate data, eliminating the API-endpoint boilerplate for your app's *internal* mutations (forms, updates), while real API route handlers serve *external* consumers. Tomorrow you build those traditional API endpoints with route handlers, for when you need a real HTTP API.

## Streaming and Suspense: fast even when data is slow
Server components fetch data on the server, but what if some data is *slow* (a slow database query, an external API)? Without help, the whole page would wait for the slowest part before *anything* shows, a blank screen while one slow query runs. **Streaming** with **Suspense** fixes this: the page sends the fast parts immediately and *streams in* the slow parts as they become ready, so the user sees content right away. You wrap a slow part in `<Suspense>` with a fallback (a loading placeholder):
```jsx
function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <Header />                              {/* fast: shows immediately */}
      <Suspense fallback={<MenuSkeleton />}>
        <Menu />                              {/* slow data: streams in when ready */}
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}
```
The header and layout appear instantly with a skeleton placeholder where the menu will be, and when the menu's data is ready, it streams in and replaces the skeleton, no blank-screen wait.

## Why streaming improves the real experience
Streaming addresses the gap between *total load time* and *perceived speed*, the same distinction as time-to-first-token in the AI track, and it is a major UX win. Without streaming, the user stares at nothing until the *slowest* piece of data is ready, so one slow query makes the *entire* page feel slow even if everything else was instant. With streaming, the page's structure and fast content appear immediately (the user sees *something* useful right away), and the slow parts fill in progressively with clear loading placeholders, so the page *feels* fast even though the slow data takes the same total time to arrive. This matters because perceived performance drives user satisfaction: a page that shows its header, nav, and fast content in 200ms and streams in a slow section over the next second feels far better than one that shows nothing for that whole second and then everything at once. Suspense's fallback (the skeleton/loading state) is key, it tells the user "this part is coming," turning a blank wait into a clear, progressive load. Streaming lets you build pages that are resilient to slow data: one slow component no longer holds the whole page hostage, it just streams in late while everything else is already usable.

## The mental model: progressive, prioritised loading
The deeper idea streaming teaches is **progressive, prioritised rendering**, sending the page in order of importance and readiness rather than all-or-nothing, which is a powerful way to think about loading any complex UI. You decide what should appear first (the layout, the critical content, the fast stuff) and let the slower, less critical parts stream in afterward, each wrapped in Suspense so it loads independently with its own placeholder. This means a complex page with several data sources of varying speed is no longer bottlenecked by its slowest source: the page is composed of independently-streaming sections, each appearing as soon as *it* is ready, in priority order. The skill is identifying which parts of a page are fast vs slow and wrapping the slow ones in Suspense boundaries so they do not block the rest, giving the user the fastest possible meaningful first view. This is the production-grade approach to data-driven pages, and it composes beautifully with server components: the server renders and streams the fast parts immediately while the slow data is still being fetched, then streams those in. Understanding streaming and Suspense, send the fast and important content first, stream the slow content in progressively with loading placeholders, completes your picture of how Next.js delivers fast, resilient, production-quality pages even when the underlying data is not uniformly fast. It is the difference between a page that is only as fast as its slowest query and one that feels fast regardless.

## Where this fits
You understand streaming and Suspense: sending a page progressively so fast content appears immediately and slow parts stream in with loading placeholders, improving perceived speed and making pages resilient to slow data, the production approach to data-driven rendering. Tomorrow you assemble the week's Next.js skills and ship a production-grade app.

## Tailwind core: the utilities you'll use constantly
Tailwind has hundreds of utilities, but a core set covers most styling, organised by what they do, and learning these patterns (not memorising every class) is the goal:
```jsx
// SPACING (the 8-pt scale): p-4 (padding 16px), px-6, py-24, m-2, gap-4, space-y-3
// LAYOUT: flex, grid, items-center, justify-between, grid-cols-3
// SIZING: w-full, max-w-md, h-screen, min-h-0
// TYPOGRAPHY: text-lg, font-bold, text-slate-700, leading-relaxed, text-center
// COLOR: bg-slate-900, text-white, border-slate-200
// BORDERS/EFFECTS: rounded-lg, border, shadow-md, opacity-90
```
The class *names* follow consistent patterns (`{property}-{value}`, like `bg-`, `text-`, `p-`, `m-`), so once you learn the system you can guess most classes. The values come from the constrained scale (the spacing numbers, the colour names with shades 50-950), so you compose from a curated, consistent set.

## Responsive and state variants: the real power
Tailwind's standout capability is handling *responsive design* and *interactive states* with simple prefixes, right in the markup, which in traditional CSS required separate media queries and pseudo-class rules:
```jsx
<div className="flex-col md:flex-row">           {/* column on mobile, row on md+ screens */}
<button className="bg-blue-500 hover:bg-blue-600 focus:ring-2">  {/* hover + focus states */}
<div className="text-black dark:text-white">     {/* different in dark mode */}
```
- **Responsive prefixes** (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`) apply a utility only at that screen size and up, so `flex-col md:flex-row` is mobile-first responsive (the W1-D4 principle) in a single class, no media query block.
- **State variants** (`hover:`, `focus:`, `active:`, `disabled:`) style interactive states inline, `hover:bg-blue-600` is the hover style right there.
- **Dark mode** (`dark:`) styles the dark variant inline, `text-black dark:text-white`.
This is genuinely powerful: responsive, stateful, theme-aware styling, all composed in the markup next to the element, where traditional CSS scattered it across media queries and pseudo-class selectors. The mobile-first responsive model and the state styling you learned the hard way in earlier weeks become concise and local with these prefixes, which is a large part of why Tailwind makes UI work fast.

## Customisation and the practical workflow
Two practical points complete the core. First, Tailwind is **customisable**: the design system (colours, spacing, fonts) is configured in a config file, so you can set Bean Forge's coffee-brown palette and use it as `bg-coffee` everywhere, the constrained system is *yours* to define, then enforced. Second, the practical workflow with utilities is to **lean on components for reuse**: yes, a styled button has many utility classes, but you write it *once* as a React `<Button>` component and reuse it, so the verbosity lives in one place and every use is clean (`<Button>Send</Button>`). This resolves the "isn't this repetitive?" worry, you do not repeat the utility soup, you encapsulate it in a component and reuse the component, which is exactly the composition you learned in React. So the real Tailwind workflow is: compose utilities to build a component, extract it as a reusable React component, and use the clean component throughout, getting Tailwind's iteration speed *and* DRY reuse. With the core utilities, the responsive/state prefixes, a customised design system, and component extraction, you can build almost any UI quickly and consistently, which is the practical competence this week delivers.

## Where this fits
You know Tailwind's core: the utility patterns ({property}-{value} on the constrained scale), the responsive (`md:`) and state (`hover:`, `dark:`) prefixes that make responsive, interactive, theme-aware styling concise and local, customisation of the design system, and the component-extraction workflow for reuse. Tomorrow you handle dark mode patterns properly, building on Tailwind's `dark:` variant.

## TanStack Query: server state, solved
**TanStack Query** (React Query) is the tool for *server state*, and it eliminates the hand-rolled fetching-caching-loading mess. Instead of `useEffect` + `useState` + manual flags, you declare *what* data you want and TanStack Query handles the rest:
```jsx
function Menu() {
  const { data, isLoading, error } = useQuery({
    queryKey: ['menu'],                       // a cache key
    queryFn: () => fetch('/api/menu').then(r => r.json()),  // how to fetch
  });
  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
  if (error) return <Error />;
  return <MenuList items={data} />;
}
```
That small hook gives you the data *plus* `isLoading` and `error` states for free, *and*, invisibly, caching, deduplication, background refetching, and staleness management. The whole category of server-state problems from yesterday is handled by this one tool.

## What you get for free, and why it matters
The value of TanStack Query is everything it does *without* you writing it, which is exactly the hard server-state problems from D2:
- **Caching:** fetched data is cached by its `queryKey`, so navigating away and back does not refetch, the data is instantly there from cache (then quietly refreshed if stale). Snappy UX, no manual cache.
- **Deduplication:** if five components request `['menu']` at once, TanStack Query fetches it *once* and shares the result, no redundant requests.
- **Loading and error states:** provided consistently (`isLoading`, `error`), so you stop hand-managing flags and your loading/error handling is uniform across the app.
- **Background refetching and staleness:** it refetches stale data automatically (e.g. when the window regains focus), keeping the UI fresh without you wiring up when-to-refetch logic (tomorrow's-style config, D6).
- **Mutations and invalidation:** for writes, `useMutation` plus *invalidating* a query refetches the affected data, so after you add a menu item, the menu list updates automatically.
Each of these is a real problem you would otherwise solve manually and badly; TanStack Query solves them all correctly, which is why it is the standard for any app that fetches server data.

## The mental shift: declare, don't orchestrate
The deeper lesson TanStack Query teaches is a *mental shift* in how you handle server data, from *imperatively orchestrating* fetches to *declaratively describing* what data you need. The old way (which beginners write) is imperative: "in this effect, fetch the data, set loading true, then set the data, then set loading false, and handle errors, and remember to refetch when X, and cache it somewhere, and avoid duplicate fetches", you orchestrate the whole lifecycle by hand, and it is verbose and bug-prone. TanStack Query is declarative: "this component *needs* the menu data (here's how to get it)", and the library handles the entire lifecycle (fetching, caching, loading/error, refetching, dedup). This is the same declarative philosophy as React itself (describe *what*, let the system handle *how*), now applied to server data, which is why it fits React so naturally and feels like the "right" way once you use it. The shift, *declare your data dependencies, let the library manage the fetching lifecycle*, is what makes server-state handling clean, and it is the realisation that server data is a *cache-synchronisation* problem (keep the client copy in sync with the server) that a dedicated tool should manage, not a manual fetch-and-store chore. Adopting TanStack Query for server state is one of the highest-leverage improvements you can make to a React app's data layer, and understanding *why* (it handles the genuinely-hard server-state problems declaratively) is what lets you use it well rather than cargo-culting it.

## Where this fits
You can manage server state with TanStack Query: declare the data you need (`useQuery`), and get caching, deduplication, loading/error states, and background refetching for free, the declarative solution to the server-state problems that manual fetching handles badly. Tomorrow you handle URL state, the category that belongs in the address bar.

## Async patterns: how Node handles I/O
Because Node is non-blocking and most server work is I/O (database, files, network), **asynchronous programming is central**, you constantly start an operation that finishes *later* and handle its result without blocking. The modern, clean way is **async/await** with **Promises**:
```js
async function getMenu() {
  const items = await db.menu.findMany();   // 'await' pauses THIS function until the query returns
  return items;                              // ...but the event loop is free to handle other requests
}
```
A **Promise** represents a value that will be available *later* (the result of an async operation), and `await` lets you write asynchronous code that *reads* like synchronous code (line by line) while remaining non-blocking under the hood. This is the same async JavaScript from the frontend (`fetch().then(...)`), now pervasive because server work is mostly I/O.

## Why async/await, and handling errors
Understanding the async toolkit and its evolution clarifies how to write clean server code:
- **Promises** are the foundation: an async operation returns a Promise that *resolves* with a value (success) or *rejects* with an error (failure), and you handle it with `.then()`/`.catch()` or, better, `await`.
- **async/await** is syntax over Promises that makes async code *linear and readable*: `const x = await somethingAsync()` reads top-to-bottom like normal code, instead of nested `.then()` callbacks (which, taken to extremes, become the infamous "callback hell" of deeply nested callbacks that early Node was plagued by). async/await is why modern Node code is clean.
- **Error handling** is essential and easy to forget: an awaited operation can *fail* (the database is down, the file is missing), and you handle that with `try/catch` around the `await`:
```js
try {
  const items = await db.menu.findMany();
} catch (err) {
  // handle the failure: log it, return an error response
}
```
Forgetting error handling on async operations is a top source of server crashes and unhandled-rejection bugs, an unhandled rejected Promise can crash the Node process, so wrapping async work in `try/catch` (and handling errors in your route handlers) is not optional. The discipline: every async operation can fail, so handle the failure.

## Why getting async right is critical on the server
The deeper reason async patterns matter *more* on the server than they did on the frontend is that the server's *correctness and stability under load* depend on them, tying directly back to the runtime model. Using async (non-blocking) operations is what keeps the event loop free to handle concurrent requests (D2's rule), so async is not just a style choice but a *requirement* for a performant server, accidentally using a synchronous (blocking) operation in a request handler freezes all other requests. And robust error handling on async operations is what keeps the server *stable*: a server handles untrusted input and unreliable dependencies (databases, external APIs that can fail), so async operations *will* sometimes reject, and unhandled rejections crash the process, taking down the whole server for *all* users, far higher stakes than a frontend error affecting one user's tab. So on the server, you write *all* I/O as non-blocking async (to keep the loop free and the server performant) *and* you handle errors on *every* async operation (to keep the server stable when dependencies fail). Mastering async/await with proper `try/catch` error handling is therefore foundational to writing Node servers that are both fast (non-blocking) and reliable (won't crash on a failed query). The frontend tolerated sloppier async; the server does not, because a server's job is to stay up and responsive for many users through unreliable I/O, and clean, error-handled async is what makes that possible. This is the practical heart of Node programming.

## Where this fits
You can write Node's pervasive asynchronous code cleanly: async/await over Promises for readable non-blocking I/O, with `try/catch` error handling on every async operation, which is essential on the server both for performance (keep the event loop free) and stability (unhandled rejections crash the process). Tomorrow you handle running the server as a real process: process management.

## Testing UI: Testing Library
Frontend components need testing too, and **Testing Library** (React Testing Library and friends) is the standard, built around exactly the right philosophy: **test components the way a *user* uses them**, not the way they're implemented. You render a component and interact with it as a user would, finding elements by what's *visible* (text, labels, roles) and asserting on what the *user sees*:
```jsx
test('shows the daily special', () => {
  render(<DailySpecial day="Monday" />);
  expect(screen.getByText('Monday Mocha')).toBeInTheDocument();   // user-visible outcome
});
test('filters menu when a category is clicked', async () => {
  render(<Menu items={items} />);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Cold' }));
  expect(screen.getByText('Cold Brew')).toBeVisible();
  expect(screen.queryByText('Hot Latte')).not.toBeInTheDocument();
});
```
You find elements by their *accessible* attributes (text, role, label, what a user perceives) and interact via realistic user events (click, type), then assert on the *visible result*, exactly as a user experiences the component.

## Why "test like a user" is the right philosophy
Testing Library's user-centric approach is the component-level embodiment of yesterday's *test behaviour, not implementation*, and it is powerful for the same reasons plus a bonus:
- **Robust to refactoring:** because you find elements by what's visible (text, role) and assert on user-visible outcomes, *not* by internal details (component state, CSS classes, internal structure), your tests survive refactoring the component's internals, exactly the behaviour-not-implementation robustness. A test that clicks the "Cold" button and checks "Cold Brew" appears keeps passing through any internal rewrite that preserves that behaviour.
- **Tests what actually matters:** users don't care about your component's internal state; they care that clicking the button shows the right thing, so testing the *user-visible behaviour* tests what actually matters, giving meaningful confidence that the component *works for users*.
- **Encourages accessibility (the bonus):** Testing Library pushes you to find elements by *accessible* attributes (role, label, text), so writing tests this way *requires* your components to be accessible (have proper roles, labels), if you can't find a button by its accessible name in a test, neither can a screen reader, so the test surfaces the accessibility problem. Testing this way thus *reinforces* the accessibility discipline from the frontend phase, a nice alignment where good testing and good accessibility go together.
So Testing Library's "test like a user" philosophy gives you component tests that are robust (survive refactoring), meaningful (test real user-facing behaviour), and accessibility-promoting, which is exactly what you want. It is the right way to test UI, and the contrast (testing internal state, querying by CSS class, asserting on implementation) produces brittle, less-meaningful tests, so the user-centric approach is strongly preferred.

## What to test in components, and what not to
The judgement of *what* component behaviour to test follows the same value-focused thinking as the pyramid: test the *meaningful behaviour* of components, especially the interactive and conditional logic. Test that a component *renders the right thing for given props/state* (the daily special shows the right special), *responds correctly to user interaction* (clicking filters the menu, submitting the form does the right thing), *handles its states* (loading, error, empty states render appropriately), and *conditional rendering* (the right thing shows under the right conditions). These are the component behaviours that matter and can break. You *don't* test trivial rendering (that a static heading shows its text, low value), implementation details (internal state values), or things better covered elsewhere (don't re-test pure logic in a component test if it's unit-tested separately). And purely visual concerns (does it *look* right) are generally not what these tests cover (that's visual/manual review or visual regression tools), Testing Library tests *behaviour and content*, not pixel appearance. So you focus component tests on the *interactive and conditional behaviour* (what happens when the user does X, what renders under condition Y), tested from the user's perspective, which gives valuable confidence that your UI behaves correctly for users while keeping the tests robust and worthwhile. This complements the unit tests (pure logic) and E2E tests (full flows): component tests cover the *component-level behaviour* in between, tested the right way.

## Where this fits
You can test UI components with Testing Library, finding elements by accessible attributes and asserting on user-visible outcomes (the "test like a user" philosophy that is robust, meaningful, and accessibility-promoting), focused on the meaningful interactive and conditional behaviour. Tomorrow you handle testing code that uses the database: test databases.

## End-to-end testing: Playwright
The top of the pyramid, **E2E tests**, verify the *whole app from the user's perspective* by driving a *real browser* through *real flows*, and **Playwright** is the modern standard tool. It automates a real browser to do what a user does, navigate, click, type, and asserts the user sees the right things:
```js
test('user can sign up and reach the dashboard', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/signup');
  await page.fill('input[name="email"]', 'new@user.com');
  await page.fill('input[name="password"]', 'secret123');
  await page.click('button:has-text("Sign up")');
  await expect(page).toHaveURL('/dashboard');
  await expect(page.getByText('Welcome')).toBeVisible();
});
```
This drives a *real browser* through the *real, deployed (or running) app* (real frontend, real backend, real database), exactly as a user would experience it, and verifies the *whole flow works end-to-end*. Playwright can test across browsers, handle the async nature of web apps (waiting for things to load), and even capture screenshots/videos of failures.

## Why E2E tests, and their place
E2E tests give a unique, valuable kind of confidence that lower tests can't, and understanding their role is key to using them well:
- **They verify the system *actually works* for users:** unit and integration tests verify pieces and connections, but only an E2E test confirms that the *entire* stack, frontend, backend, database, all integrated, works together as a *user* experiences it. It catches the bugs that only appear when everything runs together (a frontend that calls the API wrong, a flow that breaks across the full stack), the ultimate "does it actually work?" check.
- **They test the critical flows that *must* work:** you reserve E2E tests for the *handful* of most-important user journeys, sign up / log in, the core feature, checkout/payment, the flows where a break is catastrophic and that you absolutely need to know work end-to-end. These earn the expensive E2E coverage.
- **They're the slowest and most brittle, so you use them sparingly:** E2E tests are slow (a real browser doing a real flow takes seconds), expensive to write and maintain, and more *brittle* (they can fail for flaky reasons, timing, environment) than lower tests. This is *why* they're the small top of the pyramid: a *few* E2E tests on the critical flows give huge confidence, but a *lot* of E2E tests make the suite slow, flaky, and painful, so you don't test everything E2E, only the vital flows, and rely on the faster unit/integration tests for the rest.
So E2E tests with Playwright provide the top-level confidence that the *whole app works for users* on its *critical flows*, used sparingly (per the pyramid) because they're slow and brittle, complementing the many fast unit tests and some integration tests below. This three-level coverage, fast unit tests for logic, integration tests for connections, a few E2E tests for critical user journeys, is what gives comprehensive confidence efficiently.

## Writing good E2E tests: focus and robustness
The discipline for E2E tests, given their cost and brittleness, is *focus* and *robustness*. **Focus:** test only the *critical* user journeys (the ones that must work and would be catastrophic to break), not every path, because each E2E test is expensive, so you spend the budget on the vital flows (auth, core feature, payment) and let lower tests cover the rest. **Robustness:** because E2E tests can be flaky (timing issues, environment differences), you write them to be reliable, use the tool's auto-waiting (Playwright waits for elements rather than fixed sleeps, reducing flakiness), find elements by stable, user-facing attributes (like Testing Library, by visible text/role, not brittle selectors), and keep tests independent (each sets up its own state). Flaky E2E tests are a real problem (they fail randomly, eroding trust in the suite until people ignore failures), so writing them robustly matters. You also typically run E2E tests against a realistic environment (a deployed preview or a full local stack with a test database). Used this way, focused on critical flows, written robustly, E2E tests are a high-value top layer: a few reliable tests confirming the most important user journeys work end-to-end, giving you confidence to ship that no amount of lower-level testing alone provides (since only E2E exercises the fully-integrated system as a user does). The combination of the whole pyramid, with E2E used judiciously at the top, is what lets you ship changes confidently knowing both the pieces *and* the critical whole work.

## Where this fits
You can write E2E tests with Playwright, driving a real browser through critical user flows to verify the *whole integrated app works for users*, used sparingly (the small top of the pyramid) because they're slow and brittle, written with focus (critical flows only) and robustness (auto-waiting, stable selectors, independence). Tomorrow you handle a recurring testing decision: mocks vs the real thing.
